WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.979 --> 00:00:05.580
Nostalgia, depression, and cinema's greatest
imaginary friend: It's time to take a journey

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:07.300
inside the mind of Riley Andersen.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:08.300
Who knows?

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:10.950
You might even learn something about your
own mind.

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:14.320
Inside Out is centered around the hectic mind
of a child, and much of the plot involves

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:17.800
tackling the emotional disarray that characterizes
that period of life.

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:21.519
But the film also takes the time to hop into
the heads of the other people around Riley,

00:00:21.519 --> 00:00:22.640
such as her parents.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:25.980
When we get a glimpse into their Headquarters,
some interesting details are revealed about

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.780
the way maturity affects the mind.

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:31.839
In the minds of both Riley's mother and father,
the emotions operate as a much more cohesive

00:00:31.839 --> 00:00:36.290
team, sitting together around a larger control
panel accessed by every emotion.

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:40.489
The emotions work in tandem, too, with each
one contributing a little to any given situation

00:00:40.489 --> 00:00:44.899
— in sharp contrast to the incessant scramble
for control going on inside Riley's head.

00:00:44.899 --> 00:00:48.870
Even the emotions themselves are shown to
be more level-headed in the adults' heads.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:52.329
While their equivalents in Riley's mind are
shown to be driven by their respective emotions

00:00:52.329 --> 00:00:56.620
most of the time, the emotions in her parents'
Headquarters are depicted more like professionals.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:59.930
This doesn't mean that the adults aren't prone
to impulsive outbursts, but it does mean that

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:02.980
they've learned to manage those impulses over
time.

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:06.610
The question of which emotion is dominant
in Headquarters proves to be much more crucial

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:10.740
for a child, since only one emotion can operate
the controls at a time.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:14.770
Indeed, it's Joy's autocratic central role
that eventually causes everything to fall

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:15.770
apart.

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:20.409
"You can't focus on what's going wrong, there's
always a way to turn things around — to

00:01:20.409 --> 00:01:21.409
find the fun!"

00:01:21.409 --> 00:01:24.760
In adults, meanwhile, it appears that there
is a "central" emotion at Headquarters — but

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:28.400
they're more of a team coordinator, making
sure everybody's actions are aligned.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:31.710
It's never confirmed if the emotion sitting
at the center of an adult control panel is

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:35.930
constant or changes from moment to moment
as the situation dictates, but, whatever the

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:39.329
case, the emotions in charge of Riley's parents
are very revealing.

00:01:39.329 --> 00:01:42.930
In her mother's mind, the leader is Sadness
— which makes perfect sense for an adult

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:46.430
who is currently experiencing a melancholy
life change, and constantly fretting over

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:48.689
her family's emotional wellbeing.

00:01:48.689 --> 00:01:51.409
Sadness is, after all, the emotion that powers
empathy.

00:01:51.409 --> 00:01:55.430
Riley's father, meanwhile, is having his emotions
coordinated by Anger, which is also a very

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:59.439
understandable adult response to the series
of setbacks and logistical issues he's having

00:01:59.439 --> 00:02:01.700
to deal with at home and work.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:05.270
Inside Out's message essentially states that
all emotions have their place in a person's

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:06.969
development, including sadness.

00:02:06.969 --> 00:02:11.070
But a more seasoned viewer might also notice
that the film goes beyond that message, and

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:15.030
even enters more incisive thematic territory
in the way that it analyzes the role of Joy

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:16.530
in Riley's mind.

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:20.540
Joy is undeniably at fault for suppressing
Riley's more negative and nuanced emotions

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:23.860
in an attempt to keep her happy 100% of the
time.

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:27.360
But pay closer attention to the character's
actions and their consequences in the film

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:31.480
— specifically the way that Joy keeps desperately
replaying Riley's old happy memories when

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:33.989
she's reacting to the move to San Francisco.

00:02:33.989 --> 00:02:37.370
Because it's difficult for Riley to find things
to be happy about in her new situation, her

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:40.330
mind keeps clinging to old, bygone sources
of joy.

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:44.000
But those memories can't do anything for Riley
in the present, except remind her that things

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:45.340
are not the way she wants.

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:49.780
By resorting to nostalgia, Riley makes it
harder to process her current situation, and,

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:54.840
in failing to process it, she loses any chance
of forming any new happy memories.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:58.150
Arguably the most heartbreaking moment in
the movie comes when Sadness takes over Riley's

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:02.180
Headquarters just as she's returning home
to her parents, resulting in a long-overdue

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:05.610
emotional breakdown that finally allows them
to understand her pain.

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:09.370
The fact that Riley's control panel was turning
gray and inoperable before that moment has

00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:12.909
been widely interpreted as a metaphor for
depression; she is only able to break out

00:03:12.909 --> 00:03:17.060
of this when she accepts and surrenders to
the sadness her mind has been trying to suppress.

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:20.750
It's an incredibly potent insight — but
the film's depression metaphor runs even deeper

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:21.750
than that.

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:25.640
Essentially, Riley's depression is a result
of dysfunctional emotional processing.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:29.280
Her mind cannot deal with all the feelings
that come with moving to San Francisco.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.940
She may have been a very happy kid before,
but her happiness, her sense of self, and

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:38.000
her relationship with life were all contingent
on external circumstances that no longer exist.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.690
Never before had she been cast so helplessly
into a situation that left her so vulnerable

00:03:41.690 --> 00:03:44.200
to fear, rage, repulsion, and confusion.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:47.819
As a result, she shuts down emotionally, because
her mind doesn't know what else to do.

00:03:47.819 --> 00:03:49.319
"What have we done?"

00:03:49.319 --> 00:03:53.360
It's only by accepting and moving through
the pain that she's able to break out of that

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:55.980
defensive apathy and feel again.

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:59.340
In the credits, we get an insight into the
Headquarters of other minor characters in

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:03.240
the film, and each one reveals something amusing
about the character in question.

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:06.750
Riley's school teacher, depicted in a bout
of frustrated boredom at her students' disinterest

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:09.110
in her class, is shown to be led by Joy.

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:13.350
This may seem odd at first, but it makes sense:
The ability to remain cheerful makes for an

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:16.590
indispensable tool in a middle school teacher's
arsenal.

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:20.549
The perpetually annoyed cashier at the pizza
place is managed by Disgust, which tracks

00:04:20.549 --> 00:04:23.409
with her dry attitude towards her customers.

00:04:23.409 --> 00:04:27.090
Since Disgust is the emotion of social and
aesthetic judgment, it's also no surprise

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:30.820
that someone who has to deal with customers
all day would default to being led by it.

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:34.570
The cool girl at Riley's school is, of course,
ruled by the constant Fear she has of being

00:04:34.570 --> 00:04:36.169
exposed as a fraud.

00:04:36.169 --> 00:04:40.240
We even get a glimpse into the real-life Jangles
the Clown, who, despite hating his job, is

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:44.199
ruled by Joy — a nice rhyme with the schoolteacher,
as they're both adults whose professional

00:04:44.199 --> 00:04:47.810
lives revolve around keeping kids entertained
and engaged.

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:51.560
With Joy and Sadness lost somewhere in the
recesses of Riley's mind, it's up to Fear,

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:54.240
Disgust, and Anger to run Headquarters in
their absence.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:58.410
At one point, Fear gives up trying to salvage
Riley's emotional state and attempts to quit,

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:01.280
leaving Headquarters through the same tube
that sucked up Joy and Sadness.

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:03.729
"Emotions can't quit, genius.

00:05:03.729 --> 00:05:04.729
Bleugh!"

00:05:04.729 --> 00:05:07.620
The emotions can't quit, but Joy and Sadness
were very much removed from Headquarters,

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:10.199
which was functionally identical to what Fear
tried to do.

00:05:10.199 --> 00:05:14.320
This seems to suggest two things: The first
is that, while emotions can be accidentally

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:18.190
ejected from Headquarters, an emotion can't
voluntarily self-eject.

00:05:18.190 --> 00:05:21.670
Our feelings might get messy for any number
of reasons, but we're not allowed to choose

00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:22.949
which ones we get rid of.

00:05:22.949 --> 00:05:27.470
The second is that Fear can't remove itself
from our minds out of, well, fear.

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:31.030
There's an argument to be made that fear is
the most primal of all human emotions — the

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:34.210
base instinct to evade danger and keep ourselves
alive and healthy.

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:38.509
We can block out our sadness, our anger, and
even our joy, but fear isn't something that

00:05:38.509 --> 00:05:43.310
can be suppressed; it's always there to some
degree, an omnipresent participant in decision-making

00:05:43.310 --> 00:05:45.010
processes.

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:48.669
Inside Out may be a kids' movie, but it's
also a Pixar movie, and, as such, it makes

00:05:48.669 --> 00:05:51.180
at least a couple of adult-oriented jokes.

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:53.520
In this case, many of them revolve around
San Francisco.

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:57.780
The maddening traffic, the apathetic cashier
with a purple sidecut, and the broccoli pizza

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:01.820
are the obvious gags, but the funniest joke
is arguably the one that's most expressly

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:03.639
designed to go over kids' heads.

00:06:03.639 --> 00:06:04.639
"What was that?

00:06:04.639 --> 00:06:05.639
Was it a bear?"

00:06:05.639 --> 00:06:07.110
"There are no bears in San Francisco."

00:06:07.110 --> 00:06:08.460
"I saw a really hairy guy.

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:09.460
He looked like a bear."

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:13.280
To some extent, "a guy so hairy he looked
like a bear" is technically descriptive enough

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:16.740
to work as a joke even if you're unfamiliar
with a certain terminology.

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:21.650
But what's funny about the joke is the reference
to the San Francisco LGBTQ+ community, and

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:26.360
specifically to the concept of "bears" — which
is gay slang for plus-sized, hairy guys.

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:30.430
If you're an adult who's aware of the city's
status as a worldwide queer capital, the mere

00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:35.409
suggestion that there are no bears in San
Francisco will be enough to get you giggling.

00:06:35.409 --> 00:06:39.650
There are several references in Inside Out
that children would be very unlikely to get.

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:43.150
Heck, some of the references are esoteric
enough that even adult viewers might not pick

00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:44.210
up on them at first.

00:06:44.210 --> 00:06:47.580
The most memorable one is arguably the Chinatown
shout-out.

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:50.980
While two security guards are trying to solve
the case of the cloud man who was blown away

00:06:50.980 --> 00:06:55.099
by Bing Bong, Joy dashes right through the
cloud man's wife as she's giving testimony.

00:06:55.099 --> 00:06:56.560
"Was he with anyone?"

00:06:56.560 --> 00:06:58.379
"Yes, and there she is!"

00:06:58.379 --> 00:06:59.830
"Hey, come back here!"

00:06:59.830 --> 00:07:00.930
"Forget it, Jake.

00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:01.930
It's Cloudtown."

00:07:01.930 --> 00:07:06.740
This, of course, is a hilariously over-the-top
nod to the bleak ending of the classic 1974

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:07.740
detective film.

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:08.750
"Forget it, Jake.

00:07:08.750 --> 00:07:09.750
It's Chinatown."

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:13.990
At another point, when Joy, Sadness, and Bing
Bong are walking through the Dream Productions

00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:18.800
lot, they pass a poster for a film titled
I'm Falling for a Very Long Time Into a Pit;

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:23.389
this is a clear parody of Saul Bass' iconic
poster for Vertigo — a 1958 film in which

00:07:23.389 --> 00:07:26.310
James Stewart's character has a nightmare
about just that.

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:30.310
When a major character in a movie has a big,
tear-jerking death scene like that suffered

00:07:30.310 --> 00:07:34.500
by Bing Bong in Inside Out, the story will
usually later take a moment to memorialize

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:36.300
or mourn that character in some way.

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:39.730
In this film, however, the characters never
acknowledge Bing Bong after he sacrifices

00:07:39.730 --> 00:07:42.259
himself to help Joy to escape the Memory Dump.

00:07:42.259 --> 00:07:45.449
When he's forgotten by Riley and fades away,
it seems like the movie forgets about him

00:07:45.449 --> 00:07:46.449
as well.

00:07:46.449 --> 00:07:50.789
This is a decision that may feel odd and unsatisfying,
especially for younger viewers, but adult

00:07:50.789 --> 00:07:54.580
viewers will understand exactly why the movie
depicts Bing Bong's demise that way.

00:07:54.580 --> 00:07:58.300
The very source of the melancholy underlying
Bing Bong as a character is the fact that

00:07:58.300 --> 00:08:02.569
he's an anachronism, a relic of early childhood
with no place in an older mind.

00:08:02.569 --> 00:08:07.169
He's a walking reminder that childhood ends
and never comes back — and once it's gone,

00:08:07.169 --> 00:08:08.870
it's also largely forgotten.

00:08:08.870 --> 00:08:12.960
All the ideas and imaginative adventures that
make up each child's own universe fade away

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:13.960
after a while.

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:18.111
They're not mourned, they're not eulogized,
they're not missed — they just go, and so

00:08:18.111 --> 00:08:19.280
does Bing Bong.

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:23.940
A key plot point in the film is the fact that
Sadness is extremely knowledgeable about Riley's

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:28.750
mind; this is why Joy relies on her to guide
them through the vast realms outside Headquarters.

00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:32.779
The groundwork for this is laid when Sadness
reveals that she has read almost all of the

00:08:32.779 --> 00:08:36.710
"mind manuals," dozens of dense tomes detailing
the mind's workings and the way that memories

00:08:36.710 --> 00:08:38.149
are processed and stored.

00:08:38.149 --> 00:08:41.810
"Long term memory data selection via channel
subgrouping?"

00:08:41.810 --> 00:08:42.810
"See?

00:08:42.810 --> 00:08:43.810
Fun already!

00:08:43.810 --> 00:08:46.940
You lucky dog, you're reading these cool things,
I gotta get back to work."

00:08:46.940 --> 00:08:50.589
While this may appear to be a mere joke about
the fact that Sadness is left out of Headquarters

00:08:50.589 --> 00:08:54.950
operations and ends up reading a lot to pass
the time, a closer reading reveals something

00:08:54.950 --> 00:08:57.260
crucial about the film's themes.

00:08:57.260 --> 00:09:00.760
Sadness isn't just key to fostering empathy
and understanding between people, it's also

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:02.460
key to self-understanding.

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:06.980
As adults know, sadness is the emotion most
likely to be conducive to self-analysis and

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:07.980
introspection.

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:11.440
Poets and philosophers alike have described
sadness as the emotion of the resilient, the

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:14.010
mark of those capable of facing the human
condition head-on.

00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:17.860
No wonder Sadness knows the rules of Riley's
mind so well — she's the only one who has

00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:19.250
bothered to have a look at them.

00:09:19.250 --> 00:09:23.649
And, without Sadness, Joy could never have
found her way through Riley's mind.

00:09:23.649 --> 00:09:27.120
At the end of Inside Out, it's implied that
Riley has attained the hard-earned gift of

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:28.690
emotional complexity.

00:09:28.690 --> 00:09:32.630
She can now produce blended memories, and
her expanded control panel suggests that her

00:09:32.630 --> 00:09:34.810
emotions will finally be able to start working
together.

00:09:34.810 --> 00:09:38.050
But that's not the end of the movie's message
about the interplay between emotions.

00:09:38.050 --> 00:09:41.990
It doesn't seem to be a coincidence that Joy
is the only emotion that features another

00:09:41.990 --> 00:09:45.279
emotion's color in her design: deep blue,
in her hair and eyes.

00:09:45.279 --> 00:09:49.870
She's also the only one who experiences another
emotion besides her own to a blatant degree.

00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:53.820
If Riley's early childhood was spent clinging
to Joy to the detriment of other emotions,

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:58.150
Joy realizes that, as maturity comes knocking,
she'll have to start getting used to sadness.

00:09:58.150 --> 00:10:02.360
The film's ultimate point appears to be that,
as a person grows up, there comes a point

00:10:02.360 --> 00:10:05.750
when joy can only really exist insofar as
sadness does.

00:10:05.750 --> 00:10:10.030
There's inherent loneliness and despair in
the human condition, a fear of loss and displacement

00:10:10.030 --> 00:10:14.190
that follows us all through adulthood, but
there's also joy — and that joy comes as

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:15.940
a direct result of sadness.

00:10:15.940 --> 00:10:20.170
Once we stop taking our lives for granted,
the good things become even more special and

00:10:20.170 --> 00:10:22.690
beautiful, exactly because they're finite
and rare.

00:10:22.690 --> 00:10:26.880
Joy learns this, Riley learns this, and Sadness
is right there to help guide them through

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:28.440
whatever comes next.

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:29.760
Heavy stuff for a kids' movie.

